event: message
data: {"result":{"tools":[{"name":"theo_health","title":"Theo startup health","description":"Theo's resolved startup state: whether graph tools are serving or quarantined, the remediation command when quarantined, and the detected RBAC and apoc.trigger capabilities of this instance. Also reports watcher_coverage, the Framework counts this process measured in canon at startup, whose status is not-measured when the startup probe could not run. served_as says whether this process is the hosted endpoint or a local stdio process; endpoint is the public MCP URL when hosted and null otherwise; plugin_only_tools lists the operational tools that need a co-located MindrianOS plugin install and refuse with PLUGIN_MISSING on the hosted endpoint. A tool missing from tools/list while theo_health answers means served, not listed, which is a different reading from unreachable. Reads frozen startup state; runs no query.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{}},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"},"outputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"mode":{"type":"string","enum":["ok","quarantined"]},"quarantineCode":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"quarantineDetail":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"remediation":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"instanceUri":{"type":"string"},"serverAgent":{"type":"string"},"detectedAt":{"type":"string"},"readOnlyRoleAvailable":{"type":"boolean"},"apocTriggerAvailable":{"type":"boolean"},"showRolesProbe":{"type":"string"},"build_stamp":{"type":"object","properties":{"sha":{"type":"string"},"builtAt":{"type":"string"},"dirty":{"type":"boolean"}},"required":["sha","builtAt","dirty"],"additionalProperties":false},"sync_drift":{"type":"object","properties":{"baseline_stamp":{"type":"string"},"canon_stamps":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"releases_cut_since":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"payloads_applied_since":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"payloads_emitted_since":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"materiality":{"type":"string","enum":["identical","moved","not-measured"]},"finding":{"type":"string","enum":["within-threshold","beyond-threshold","not-measured"]},"measured_at":{"type":"string"}},"required":["baseline_stamp","canon_stamps","releases_cut_since","payloads_applied_since","payloads_emitted_since","materiality","finding","measured_at"],"additionalProperties":false},"watcher_coverage":{"type":"object","properties":{"frameworks_with_watcher":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"frameworks_total":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"frameworks_with_problem_type":{"anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]},"status":{"type":"string","enum":["measured","not-measured"]}},"required":["frameworks_with_watcher","frameworks_total","frameworks_with_problem_type","status"],"additionalProperties":false},"served_as":{"type":"string","enum":["hosted","stdio"]},"endpoint":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"null"}]},"plugin_only_tools":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["mode","quarantineCode","quarantineDetail","remediation","instanceUri","serverAgent","detectedAt","readOnlyRoleAvailable","apocTriggerAvailable","showRolesProbe","build_stamp","sync_drift","watcher_coverage","served_as","endpoint","plugin_only_tools"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"classify_problem_type","title":"The PWS problem-type catalog","description":"Returns the four PWS problem types -- UnDefined, IllDefined, WellDefined and Wicked -- each with its label, the number of journey Phases mapped to it, and the number of chapters reachable through those Phases. TAKES NO PARAMETERS: it hands back the categories and their live evidence, and the caller does the classifying. Trinity and Compass exist in the graph as DomainConcept meta-categories but are deliberately not returned, because neither carries a Phase and offering one as a problem type would offer a choice that leads nowhere. coverage.matched is how many of the returned problem types have at least one Phase mapped to them; coverage.total is the live number of problem-type nodes returned, counted on this call rather than assumed.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"normalize_taxonomy_term","title":"Resolve a term to canonical handles","description":"Resolves a short term to the canonical handles it could mean, across all four handle labels: Chapter, Phase, ToolType and DomainConcept. A problem-type spelling such as ill defined, Ill-Defined or illdefined also resolves to its canonical DomainConcept id (case, spaces, hyphens and underscores folded). Returns up to 10 candidates, each with its kind, canonicalId and canonicalLabel, ordered by id. THIS IS THE FRONT DOOR OF THE CATALOG: when you hold a human phrase rather than a handle, resolve it here first, then call the typed tool with the canonicalId you picked. Candidates are offered, never chosen -- the caller decides which one it meant. term takes a TERM, not a question: at most three words, 2 to 48 characters, letters, digits, spaces, hyphens and apostrophes only. A longer phrase is refused before the query runs. coverage.matched is how many handles resolved for this term, capped at 10; coverage.total is the live number of canonical handles across the four labels, counted on this call. Zero candidates with a non-zero total is a real answer: the term is not in the taxonomy.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"term":{"type":"string","minLength":2,"maxLength":48,"pattern":"^[\\p{L}\\p{N} '-]+$","description":"A short term or handle, at most three words."}},"required":["term"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"taxonomy_ladder","title":"The PWS problem ladder, with one rung marked","description":"Renders the four-rung PWS problem ladder as text with exactly one rung marked. THE CALLER CLASSIFIES: pass the rung you already decided on, silently, and this tool marks it. It never classifies, never infers a rung and never defaults to one. The four rungs, in this order: UnDefined, the future-of-X question, where no boundary exists yet and the work is bounding it; IllDefined, the next-big-thing-in-X question, bounded but with the real problem still hiding; WellDefined, a falsifiable spec to execute and measure; Wicked, where no rung holds it because stakeholders define the problem differently and the work is surfacing the tensions. Returns rung, the id you passed; question_label, your paraphrase echoed back or null; rungs, the four rungs each with its id, its gloss and whether it is marked; and ladder, the rendered text. Reads no graph, so its answer never varies with canon and never goes stale between calls. The rung ids are the same four ids classify_problem_type returns, so the two tools compose without transliterating between two spellings of one taxonomy.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"rung":{"type":"string","enum":["UnDefined","IllDefined","WellDefined","Wicked"],"description":"The canon DomainConcept id of the rung YOU classified the question onto. One of the four ids classify_problem_type returns. This tool marks it and does not decide it."},"question_label":{"description":"Optional. A SHORT PARAPHRASE of the question, at most 120 characters. Never verbatim user content and never room content: Canon Part 8 keeps room bytes off every content tool. It is used only to compose the last line of the returned ladder text and to echo back, and it goes nowhere else.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":120}},"required":["rung"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"find_frameworks_for_problem_type","title":"Find the chapters that address a problem type","description":"Returns the book chapters that address one of the four PWS problem types, traversed as (:DomainConcept)<-[:ADDRESSES]-(:Phase)<-[:PART_OF]-(:Chapter), with each chapter carrying its phase and its tool types. LIMITS, stated rather than implied: the four mapped phases reach 9 of the 33 chapters, so coverage.status reads partial on every call and that is the correct live measurement, not a caveat -- an unmapped chapter is genuinely not addressed by any problem type in the graph. coverage.matched is how many distinct chapters this problem type reaches; coverage.total is how many chapters exist. Accepts only the four problem types: Trinity and Compass are DomainConcept meta-categories carrying no phase, not problem types, and are refused. Resolve free text through normalize_taxonomy_term before calling.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"problem_type":{"description":"The canonical DomainConcept id for one of the four PWS problem types. Case, spaces, hyphens and underscores are folded, so ill defined, Ill-Defined and illdefined all read as IllDefined; Trinity and Compass are meta-categories, not problem types. Resolve other free text through normalize_taxonomy_term first.","type":"string","enum":["UnDefined","IllDefined","WellDefined","Wicked"]}},"required":["problem_type"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"find_chapter_for_need","title":"Find the chapters that serve a kind of need","description":"Returns the chapters the book classifies under one tool type, each with its id, label and source file, ordered by chapter id. tool_type is a closed six-value vocabulary -- Decision, Structure, Reframe, Evidence, Complexity, Gate -- and is case sensitive; anything else, including a described need in prose, is refused before the query runs. To go from a human phrase to one of these six, call normalize_taxonomy_term first. IMPORTANT: this tool only reaches chapters carrying an INSTANCE_OF edge to a tool type, and 15 of the 33 chapters carry none, so a thin or empty answer here is expected rather than anomalous -- when it comes back thin, escalate to search_theo_chunks_fulltext with the need described in plain terms. coverage.matched is how many chapters carry this tool type; coverage.total is the live size of the whole chapter corpus, counted on this call.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"tool_type":{"type":"string","enum":["Decision","Structure","Reframe","Evidence","Complexity","Gate"],"description":"One of the six ToolType ids, exactly as spelled."}},"required":["tool_type"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"chapter_sequence","title":"Chapter reading sequence","description":"Returns the chapters adjacent to one chapter in the book sequence, as four separately typed lists: comesBefore and precedesNext (PRECEDES), requiresFirst (PREREQUISITE) and feedsInto (PRODUCES_INPUT_FOR). Takes a canonical Chapter id, never a question or a title -- resolve free text through normalize_taxonomy_term first. coverage.matched is how many distinct chapters this one reaches across all four relations; coverage.total is how many chapters carry any sequence relation at all, which is fewer than the whole corpus because many chapters are unsequenced. Four empty lists means this chapter is unsequenced; zero rows means no chapter carries that id.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"chapter_id":{"type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{2,64}$","description":"A canonical Chapter id such as ch03 or feynman, resolvable through normalize_taxonomy_term. Letters, digits, underscore and hyphen only."}},"required":["chapter_id"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"theo_neighborhood","title":"Read one chapter in full","description":"Returns every canon section of ONE chapter -- HOOK, CONCEPT, FRAMEWORK, CASE, EXERCISE, SYNTHESIS -- with full text, always in that order, plus missingSections naming any of the six the chapter does not carry, frameworks anchored to the chapter (name plus orchestrationStatus, draft or active), processSteps reached through that Framework two or three hops out (step and order, plus stage when the Framework uses a Stage tier, otherwise null), and techniques reached the same way (name only). An empty frameworks/processSteps/techniques list is a normal answer for a chapter whose Framework has not been structurally decomposed, or that has no Framework at all. Takes a canonical Chapter id, never a question: this tool reads a chapter you have already identified. To find the chapter first, run search_theo_chunks_fulltext and read chapterId off the top hits, or resolve a name through normalize_taxonomy_term. Nothing is ranked and nothing is dropped. Zero rows means no chapter carries that id.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"chapter_id":{"type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{2,64}$","description":"A canonical Chapter id such as ch01 or bono, resolvable through normalize_taxonomy_term. Letters, digits, underscore and hyphen only."}},"required":["chapter_id"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"search_theo_chunks_fulltext","title":"Search the book chunks by keyword","description":"Ranked keyword search over the book text. Returns up to 8 chunks, each with its chapterId, section, relevance score and a 200-character snippet. Takes SEARCH TERMS, not a question and not a query expression: search-grammar metacharacters, field prefixes such as `section:HOOK`, a leading `-`, and bare AND / OR / NOT are all refused with a named VALIDATION_FAILED error rather than silently returning nothing. Terms are combined with OR by default, so no operator is needed -- `innovation problem framework` and `innovation OR problem OR framework` match identically. Letters in any script are accepted together with their accent and vowel marks. A character that is neither usable in a term nor search grammar (an emoji, a symbol, an invisible direction mark) is refused with a named VALIDATION_FAILED unsupported-character error giving its code point, and a mark with no letter before it, or a query with no letter or digit at all, is refused the same way rather than silently returning nothing. When the searched terms contain letters in a non-Latin writing system, the answer also carries script_coverage: one entry per such script (script is one of Hebrew, Arabic, Cyrillic, Greek, Devanagari, Han, Hiragana, Katakana, Hangul, Thai or Other) with status in_corpus, not_in_corpus or unmeasured. not_in_corpus is a live count (chunks_with_script 0 of chunks_total) showing the book text holds no letters in that script at all: an empty or English-only result then means the LANGUAGE is not covered, not that the topic is absent, and English search terms are the way to test the topic. in_corpus gives the live number of chunks that do contain the script. unmeasured, with unmeasured_reason no_pattern_for_script or coverage_query_failed, means coverage for that script is unknown, never zero. script_coverage is absent when every letter searched is Latin, and then an empty row set means the terms are genuinely not in the book.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":256,"description":"Search TERMS, space separated. Letters in any script (with their accent and vowel marks), digits, spaces and hyphens only."}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"command_neighborhood","title":"Read one MindrianOS command in full","description":"Returns the synced registry facts for ONE /mos: command: kind (meta, utility, methodology, mechanical), surface (navigator, internal), autonomousSafe, jtbdLabel, frameworks it uses, reaches it is wired to with their posture and decisionSurface, sensors it DECLARES with their declarationSide (a command-side claim read from the connector registry, not a statement about engine behaviour), nextInRecipe and previousInRecipe with recipe name and stepIndex, feedsInto with the target label, and downstreamFrameworks (two hops: what the frameworks this command uses themselves feed into). Takes one command slug such as /mos:bono, never a question. An empty frameworks list means that command has no RESOLVED framework, which is a normal answer. An empty sensors list means that command carries no command-side sensor declaration, which is likewise a normal answer and is not a claim that no sensor covers it. Zero rows means no command carries that slug.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"command":{"type":"string","pattern":"^\\/mos:[a-z0-9-]{2,64}$","description":"One MindrianOS command slug, formatted /mos:name, such as /mos:bono. Lowercase letters, digits and hyphens after the prefix."}},"required":["command"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"framework_neighborhood","title":"Read one Framework in full","description":"Returns ONE Framework by its exact canonical name, whatever anchors it carries: brainRecords (SOURCED_FROM provenance, present on every Brain-ingested Framework), chapters (PART_OF, present only on a PWS-Book Framework), and commands (inbound USES_FRAMEWORK from /mos: commands). An empty chapters or commands list is a NORMAL answer, not a failure: 126 of the 149 live Frameworks were ingested from the Brain and carry provenance only. Aliases resolve: an aliased duplicate name returns its own row plus frameworkIdentities naming the canonical it resolves to, and a resolution the graph does not support is reported under frameworkIdentities.refusals rather than as an error. Takes an exact name, never a question and never a fragment. Zero rows means no Framework carries that exact name -- try normalize_taxonomy_term, or a different spelling. Each row also carries the Framework's core properties (description, category, frameworkType, applicableStages, patternType) and its stored pattern scores as patternScores {linear, cyclical, matrix, hierarchical}, each read raw and null when canon holds none: a null score means not scored, never scored zero, and most Frameworks carry no scores at all. This is the answer to the Brain's load_framework (MIGRATE-02).","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"framework":{"type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The exact canonical name of ONE Framework, such as Ansoff Matrix. Up to 128 characters. Matched exactly, never as a fragment."}},"required":["framework"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"framework_step","title":"Read one Framework's runnable steps","description":"Returns ONE Framework by its exact canonical name together with its ProcessSteps, ordered by their position in the source chapter. Each step carries stepId, label, stepKind (STEP, DEFINITION or ASIDE), runIt, thinkingMode, researchDirective, artifactRubric and its grounded source span. An EMPTY steps list is a NORMAL answer and covers most Frameworks in canon: only a handful have been broken into steps so far, and an empty list means exactly that, never a failure. A DEFINITION or ASIDE step is not runnable and carries no runIt instruction. sourceOrder is the extractor counter and is NOT a sequence: it restarts inside a single Framework, so use the returned list order and never sort by it. Pass step_id to narrow to exactly one step. Aliases resolve: an aliased duplicate name returns its own row plus frameworkIdentities naming the canonical it resolves to, and a resolution the graph does not support is reported under frameworkIdentities.refusals rather than as an error. Takes an exact name, never a question and never a fragment. Zero rows means no Framework carries that exact name.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"framework":{"type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The exact canonical name of ONE Framework, such as Six Thinking Hats. Up to 128 characters. Matched exactly, never as a fragment."},"step_id":{"description":"Optional. The full composed id of ONE step, such as bono::six-thinking-hats::p07, to return that step alone. Omit to get every step. This is an exact id and never an ordinal: the extractor counter restarts inside a single Framework, so an ordinal has no single meaning.","type":"string","pattern":"^[a-z0-9]{1,64}::[a-z0-9]{1,64}(?:-[a-z0-9]{1,64}){0,8}::(?:s\\d{2}(?:::p\\d{2})?|p\\d{2}|t\\d{2})$"}},"required":["framework"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"find_connections","title":"Find the bounded paths canon holds between two Frameworks","description":"Takes TWO Framework names, resolves each (exact name first, then the whole name with case ignored, then through the alias layer), and returns every shortest route canon holds between them within 3 hops. A route never passes through a provenance record (SOURCED_FROM edges and BrainRecord nodes record where a node was ingested from, not how two frameworks relate), and each distinct route is returned once. Each returned path carries four aligned keys: path (the identifier of each hop in order), pathLabels (the graph label of each hop, so pathLabels[i] describes path[i] and a Framework hop is distinguishable from a problem-type rung), edges (the relationship type joining each consecutive pair, so edges has exactly one fewer entry than path), and hops (the number of relationships, always 3 or fewer). No element of any path is ever null. The hop bound is FIXED at 3 and cannot be changed by a caller. limit is optional, defaults to 5 and is capped at 10; a larger value is refused rather than quietly reduced. AN EMPTY paths LIST IS A REAL ANSWER: both endpoints exist and canon holds no route between them within the bound. That is a DIFFERENT fact from an endpoint that does not exist, which returns NO paths key at all plus a refusals entry naming which of the two failed and why. coverage reports how many of the two names resolved. This answers over THEO'S OWN canon, which is not the Brain's graph, so paths may legitimately differ from a Brain answer for the same two names. Every answer, including a refusal, carries backend: theo-canon, which identifies which graph answered (Theo's own canon).","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"from":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The name of the Framework to start from, such as Six Thinking Hats. Resolved by exact match, then by a whole-name match with case ignored, and by following ALIAS_OF; never by fuzzy or partial match. A name matching two Frameworks once case is ignored is refused as ambiguous rather than guessed."},"to":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The name of the Framework to end at. Same resolution rules as from. A name nothing carries is reported as a named refusal, never as a fuzzy nearest hit."},"limit":{"description":"How many paths to return. Optional; defaults to 5, maximum 10. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":10}},"required":["from","to"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"brain_stats","title":"Count canon live","description":"Returns the live size of the graph: total nodes, total relationships, and a per-label breakdown ordered by label name, every number counted on this call and none of them remembered. Takes no parameters at all -- any key is refused before the query runs. Use it to check what canon holds right now rather than quoting a figure from a planning document, and to tell a genuinely thin layer of the graph apart from a query that matched nothing. The label breakdown covers every label brain_schema lists: each entry carries count and declared, where declared is true when the database label catalog lists the label. A label that is declared but carried by no node appears with count 0 and declared true, so declared-but-empty is stated rather than left out; declared false marks a label found on nodes that the catalog does not list. The node total is counted independently of the label breakdown, so an unlabelled node is included in nodes and absent from labels; a gap between the two is a real finding about the graph, not a rounding artefact.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"search","title":"Search the book by keyword, with a result count","description":"Ranked keyword search over the book text. Returns up to topK chunks, each with its chapterId, section, relevance score and a 200-character snippet. Takes SEARCH TERMS, not a question and not a query expression: search-grammar metacharacters, field prefixes such as `section:HOOK`, a leading `-`, and bare AND / OR / NOT are all refused with a named VALIDATION_FAILED error rather than silently returning nothing. Terms are combined with OR by default, so no operator is needed. topK is optional, defaults to 8, and is capped at 20 -- a larger value is refused rather than quietly reduced. Letters in any script are accepted together with their accent and vowel marks. A character that is neither usable in a term nor search grammar (an emoji, a symbol, an invisible direction mark) is refused with a named VALIDATION_FAILED unsupported-character error giving its code point, and a mark with no letter before it, or a query with no letter or digit at all, is refused the same way rather than silently returning nothing. When the searched terms contain letters in a non-Latin writing system, the answer also carries script_coverage: one entry per such script (script is one of Hebrew, Arabic, Cyrillic, Greek, Devanagari, Han, Hiragana, Katakana, Hangul, Thai or Other) with status in_corpus, not_in_corpus or unmeasured. not_in_corpus is a live count (chunks_with_script 0 of chunks_total) showing the book text holds no letters in that script at all: an empty or English-only result then means the LANGUAGE is not covered, not that the topic is absent, and English search terms are the way to test the topic. in_corpus gives the live number of chunks that do contain the script. unmeasured, with unmeasured_reason no_pattern_for_script or coverage_query_failed, means coverage for that script is unknown, never zero. script_coverage is absent when every letter searched is Latin, and then an empty row set means the terms are genuinely not in the book.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":256,"description":"Search TERMS, space separated. Letters in any script (with their accent and vowel marks), digits, spaces and hyphens only."},"topK":{"description":"How many ranked chunks to return. Optional; defaults to 8, maximum 20. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"normalize_framework_name","title":"Resolve a Framework name to its canonical spelling","description":"Takes ONE rough Framework name and returns the canonical name canon carries for it, plus matched_via -- exact when the name is already canonical, alias when an ALIAS_OF edge was followed. Matching is EXACT or by alias, never fuzzy: this is a resolver, not a spell-checker. A name nothing carries is a SUCCESS with no canonical key at all and coverage.status empty, never an error -- and the refusal block says which kind of nothing it was, so a name that simply does not exist is distinguishable from a name whose alias graph is broken. coverage.total is the live count of Frameworks in canon, counted on this call. Use this before framework_neighborhood, which takes an exact name and nothing else.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"raw":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":256,"description":"One Framework name as the caller has it, canonical or not. Resolved by exact match or by following ALIAS_OF, never by fuzzy match."}},"required":["raw"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"feeds_into_chains","title":"Walk the curated FEEDS_INTO chain layer from named seeds","description":"Takes up to 10 seed names (a Framework name or a /mos: command slug) and walks the curated FEEDS_INTO chain layer outward, at most max_hops hops (maximum 3). Returns chains, each a list of steps carrying from, to, the edge confidence and the edge transform where the graph records them, plus chain_confidence -- the product of the per-edge confidences, OMITTED entirely when any edge on the chain has no recorded confidence rather than assumed to be 1. coverage says how much of the chain layer this answer touched: matched is the number of distinct FEEDS_INTO edges the returned chains traversed and total is the live size of the layer, both counted on this call. This layer is genuinely thin, so an empty chains list with coverage.status empty is a COMMON and CORRECT answer, never an error: it means these seeds reach nothing, and coverage.total tells you whether the layer has anything to reach at all.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"seeds":{"minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128},"description":"Between 1 and 10 exact node names to start from -- a Framework name such as Ansoff Matrix, or a command slug such as /mos:bono. Matched exactly, never as a fragment. A seed nothing carries simply contributes no chains."},"max_hops":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":3,"description":"How many FEEDS_INTO hops to walk, 1 to 3. Required: there is no safe default, since a smaller value truncates the answer and a larger one costs more. A value above 3 is refused, never clamped."}},"required":["seeds","max_hops"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"orchestration_readiness","title":"Score one Framework 0-4 on orchestration readiness","description":"Takes ONE Framework name, resolves it through the alias layer, and returns a 0-4 score that is the plain SUM of four named inputs, each answered by a live query in this same call: has_structure (the Framework contains process steps or stages), has_ordering (LEADS_TO edges run between ITS OWN components), has_technique (a Technique is linked), and pattern_known. evidence carries the raw counts each boolean came from, so the score is checkable rather than trusted. pattern_known is listed in unsynced_inputs: the plugin-side orchestration projection is NOT synced into Theo, so this input reports Theo's own orchestration_status instead and is false for almost every Framework. A name nothing carries is a SUCCESS with NO score key at all and coverage.status empty, never an error, and the refusal block says which kind of nothing it was. coverage.total is the live Framework count.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"framework_name":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The name of ONE Framework, such as Six Thinking Hats. Resolved by exact match or by following ALIAS_OF, never by fuzzy match. Use normalize_framework_name first if the spelling is uncertain."}},"required":["framework_name"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"discover_structure","title":"Read one Framework structural components in sequence","description":"Takes ONE Framework name, resolves it through the alias layer, and returns its structural components as steps. THE SEQUENCE COMES FROM LIVE LEADS_TO EDGES between those components, walked on this call -- no sequencing property is read and nothing is written. Each step carries ordered: true when its position came from that walk, and ordered: false when the component sits on no walked chain, in which case its position is a listing index only. The ordering block publishes the counts the walk was derived from, including branching, so the sequence is checkable rather than trusted. COVERAGE IS THIN AND SAYS SO: only a handful of live Frameworks carry structure at all, coverage.total counts them live, and steps: [] on a real Framework is the COMMON answer rather than a failure. A name nothing carries is a SUCCESS with NO steps key at all and coverage.status empty, never an error, and the refusal block says which kind of nothing it was.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"framework_name":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The name of ONE Framework, such as The Feynman Test. Resolved by exact match or by following ALIAS_OF, never by fuzzy match. Use normalize_framework_name first if the spelling is uncertain."}},"required":["framework_name"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"recommend_chain","title":"Recommend a Framework chain for one problem type","description":"Takes a problem type and a step budget, and returns the Frameworks canon actually links to that type through ADDRESSES_PROBLEM_TYPE, ranked by the edge rank when one was recorded, then by the stored PageRank, then by live Framework degree, then by name, and capped at max_steps. AN UNKNOWN PROBLEM TYPE IS NEVER AN ERROR: it returns a success carrying chain: [], coverage.status empty, and available_problem_types listing the live problem-type ids it could have matched (the four problem types only; the Trinity and Compass meta-categories are never listed, matched or counted). problem_type is resolved by folding case, spaces, hyphens and underscores against those four ids -- resolve other wording through normalize_taxonomy_term first. The evidence block counts, live, how many Frameworks are linked to this type and how many are linked to any type, which is what separates a type that matched nothing from a link layer that holds nothing. Stored PageRank is read, never computed: it is Theo's own offline recompute, stored by a reviewed canon write. Frameworks without a stored score follow those with one, then live degree, then name, break the remaining ties, so until the first recompute is written the order is by degree. degree is a live relationship count and rides on each step so the sequence is checkable. A step whose framework canon pairs with a watcher also carries watchers, a list of that watcher name and the one-sentence reason for the pairing; THE KEY IS ABSENT ENTIRELY when the framework has no watcher, never an empty list, and evidence reports frameworks_with_watcher out of frameworks_total so you can tell a framework with no watcher apart from a watcher layer holding nothing. The response's ranking key names the ordering rule used, so a change of rule is visible on the wire rather than silent.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"problem_type":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":64,"description":"A problem type: one of UnDefined, IllDefined, WellDefined or Wicked. Case, spaces, hyphens and underscores are folded, so ill defined, Ill-Defined and illdefined all read as IllDefined; Trinity and Compass are meta-categories, not problem types. A value nothing matches returns an empty chain rather than an error."},"max_steps":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":6,"description":"How many steps the chain may hold, 1 to 6. Required: a step budget has no defensible default."}},"required":["problem_type","max_steps"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"brain_query","title":"Execute caller-authored read-only Cypher, bounded","description":"Runs read-only Cypher you have ALREADY AUTHORED against the teaching graph, behind the full guard stack. It translates nothing and interprets nothing: you hold the model and write the query. Writes are refused BEFORE execution, on the server's own EXPLAIN classification of your query plus an operator and procedure allow-list, so a rejected query never runs and never opens a session. An answer larger than the row cap (100 rows) or the byte cap (262144 bytes) is NOT refused: it comes back as a success cut off at the cap and flagged `truncated: true` with `row_cap` or `byte_cap` and `returned`, so check `truncated` before treating the rows as complete, and add a LIMIT at or under the row cap when you need every row accounted for. A query that runs past the timeout is refused with TIMEOUT. The two refusal codes mean different things: PLAN_REJECTED means a plan was produced and the policy refused it, so the fix is a DIFFERENT query; PLAN_UNAVAILABLE means no plan could be produced at all, so the fix is a CORRECT query. The answer is Theo's standard payload object carrying rows and diagnostics, NOT a bare array of records. This tool pays one extra round trip that the purpose-built tools do not, so prefer a named tool when one fits and treat this as the escape hatch.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"cypher":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":4000,"description":"Already-authored read-only Cypher. Use $-prefixed parameters, never literals."},"params":{"default":{},"description":"The query parameters, matching the $-names used in the Cypher.","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["cypher"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"},"outputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"truncated":{"description":"Present, and true, only when a cap fired and rows were cut off.","type":"boolean","const":true},"row_cap":{"description":"The row cap that fired. Set only on a row-cap truncation.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"byte_cap":{"description":"The byte cap that fired. Set only on a byte-cap truncation.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"returned":{"description":"How many rows this truncated answer carries.","type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"rows":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"diagnostics":{"type":"object","properties":{"hasWarning":{"type":"boolean"},"notifications":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"gqlStatusObjects":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"warningGqlStatuses":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["hasWarning","notifications","gqlStatusObjects","warningGqlStatuses"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["rows","diagnostics"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"brain_schema","title":"Report the live graph schema","description":"Reports the graph schema as it stands right now: the labels, the relationship types and the property keys the live graph carries, each list sorted. Takes no arguments. labels is the database label catalog, so it can include a label that no node currently carries (for example one a constraint still names); brain_stats reports every label listed here, each such label with count 0 and declared true. property_keys lists the keys ACTUALLY IN USE on nodes and relationships, not the database key token store, which also holds every key ever created and never releases one; evidence.property_key_tokens_total reports that larger number so the difference is visible rather than hidden. Every list is read on the call, so a schema change is visible immediately and no answer is served from a memorized summary. This is METADATA only: no property values and no canon content are returned. Reach for brain_stats when the question is how much the graph holds rather than what shapes it holds.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"brain_search","title":"Keyword search the book text","description":"KEYWORD SEARCH, not similarity search. Ranked lexical search over the book text through the graph fulltext index, returning each matching chunk with its chapterId, section, relevance score and a 200-character snippet. Every answer carries search_mode: lexical, which is a statement about what actually ran: matching is on the words themselves, so a query that shares no words with the book returns nothing even when it shares the meaning. Loose input is welcome: punctuation and search grammar are stripped to plain terms, and query_terms reports exactly what was searched. Terms are combined with OR, so no operator is needed. topK is optional, defaults to 8 and is applied at 20 at most; effective_top_k always reports the value used. namespace is accepted for compatibility and ignored, because there is one graph and one index to search. When the searched terms contain letters in a non-Latin writing system, the answer also carries script_coverage: one entry per such script (script is one of Hebrew, Arabic, Cyrillic, Greek, Devanagari, Han, Hiragana, Katakana, Hangul, Thai or Other) with status in_corpus, not_in_corpus or unmeasured. not_in_corpus is a live count (chunks_with_script 0 of chunks_total) showing the book text holds no letters in that script at all: an empty or English-only result then means the LANGUAGE is not covered, not that the topic is absent, and English search terms are the way to test the topic. in_corpus gives the live number of chunks that do contain the script. unmeasured, with unmeasured_reason no_pattern_for_script or coverage_query_failed, means coverage for that script is unknown, never zero. script_coverage is absent when every letter searched is Latin, and then an empty row set means the terms are genuinely not in the book.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":1024,"description":"What to search for, in plain words. Punctuation and search-index grammar are removed before the search runs, and query_terms reports what was searched."},"namespace":{"description":"Accepted for compatibility and IGNORED. Theo has one graph and one index, so there is no partition to select.","type":"string","maxLength":128},"topK":{"description":"How many ranked chunks to return. Optional; defaults to 8. Values above 20 are reduced to that, and effective_top_k reports the value actually used.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":50}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"brain_ask","title":"Structured rows from the teaching graph, in two modes","description":"RETURNS STRUCTURED ROWS, NEVER A COMPOSED ANSWER. This tool holds no model and writes no prose: you compose the answer from what it returns. Two modes, and exactly one per call. SEND question to search the book text by keyword: the answer carries answer_mode structured_rows plus ranked chunks, each with its chapterId, section, relevance score and a 200-character snippet. Matching is LEXICAL, on the words themselves, so a question sharing no words with the book returns nothing even when it shares the meaning; query_terms reports exactly what was searched after punctuation was stripped. When the searched terms contain letters in a non-Latin writing system, the answer also carries script_coverage: one entry per such script (script is one of Hebrew, Arabic, Cyrillic, Greek, Devanagari, Han, Hiragana, Katakana, Hangul, Thai or Other) with status in_corpus, not_in_corpus or unmeasured. not_in_corpus is a live count (chunks_with_script 0 of chunks_total) showing the book text holds no letters in that script at all: an empty or English-only result then means the LANGUAGE is not covered, not that the topic is absent, and English search terms are the way to test the topic. in_corpus gives the live number of chunks that do contain the script. unmeasured, with unmeasured_reason no_pattern_for_script or coverage_query_failed, means coverage for that script is unknown, never zero. script_coverage is absent when every letter searched is Latin, and then an empty row set means the terms are genuinely not in the book. SEND op to run one of three fixed queries: list_frameworks returns every framework with its category and how many names alias to it; framework_edges returns typed edges leaving the framework layer, optionally narrowed by params.edge_type or anchored on params.framework; framework_chain_slice walks the curated FEEDS_INTO layer from params.seeds and returns reachable endpoints with their hop distance. Each op answer carries coverage, where matched is what you were given and total is how many exist for your filters, so you can tell whether raising params.limit would show more. An empty row set is an ANSWER, not a failure: it means the graph holds nothing for this input.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"question":{"description":"PLAIN MODE. What to look for, in plain words. Punctuation and search-index grammar are removed before the search runs, and query_terms reports what was searched. Mutually exclusive with op.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":1024},"op":{"description":"OP MODE. One of exactly three fixed server-side queries. Any other value is refused before the handler runs. Mutually exclusive with question.","type":"string","enum":["list_frameworks","framework_edges","framework_chain_slice"]},"params":{"description":"OP MODE arguments. Sealed: an unrecognised key is refused rather than ignored, because these are arguments to queries this server wrote.","type":"object","properties":{"framework":{"description":"framework_edges only. An exact Framework name to anchor on. Resolved through ALIAS_OF first, so an alias name reaches its canonical edges.","type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$"},"edge_type":{"description":"framework_edges only. One relationship type to narrow to, such as FEEDS_INTO or ADDRESSES_PROBLEM_TYPE. Omit for every type.","type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{0,63}$"},"seeds":{"description":"framework_chain_slice only, and REQUIRED for it. Between 1 and 10 exact node names to start from: a Framework name such as Dominant Design, or a command slug such as /mos:find-bottlenecks. Matched exactly.","minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128}},"max_hops":{"description":"framework_chain_slice only. How many FEEDS_INTO hops to walk, 1 to 3. Defaults to 3.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":3},"limit":{"description":"How many rows to return. Optional; defaults to 50, maximum 200. A larger value is refused, never quietly reduced.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":200}},"additionalProperties":false},"topK":{"description":"PLAIN MODE only. How many ranked chunks to return. Optional; defaults to 8, maximum 20.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":20}},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"brain_write","title":"Write Cypher to canon (not served)","description":"NOT SERVED. Theo answers every call to this tool with a WRITE_PATH_DISABLED refusal and never executes anything: no query is parsed, no session is opened and the argument is never read. Canon is written only through the governed, reviewed payload path, which requires a drafted payload, stated evidence, human approval and a one-shot apply. This tool exists so the refusal is explicit, informative and testable rather than an accident of a name nobody implemented. Every read tool in the catalog remains available.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"cypher":{"description":"Accepted by the schema so the call reaches this tool and receives a named refusal. It is never parsed, never executed and never logged.","type":"string"}},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"schema_mapping_fit","title":"Score one local candidate against one proposed Theo target","description":"Takes ONE local type token, the kind it is (node or edge), and ONE proposed Theo target, and returns a 0-4 score that is the plain unweighted SUM of four named inputs: label_exists (the target is in the LIVE set for its kind), contract_declared (the connectivity contract knows the target), vocabulary_legal (the target is in Theo's DECLARED vocabulary) and anchor_satisfiable (the candidate could be anchored at creation through a tier-1 clause). evidence carries the raw live AND declared sets, which disagree in both directions today, so the score is checkable rather than trusted. contract_slice is the ONE proposed target entry, never the whole table. This tool MEASURES and never judges: it emits no mapping_fidelity, no threshold and no verdict. caller_declared_inputs names every input Theo did not measure, because Theo cannot read the local room graph at all. A target no live label or relationship type carries is a SUCCESS with NO score key at all and a named refusal, never an error and never a zero. Nothing is written.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"local_type":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":64,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$","description":"The local node type or edge type TOKEN, exactly as the room graph spells it. A type NAME only. Never a rationale, note, description or any room content."},"local_kind":{"type":"string","enum":["node","edge"],"description":"Whether the local candidate is a node type or an edge type. This decides which LIVE set the proposed target is resolved against."},"proposed_theo_label":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":64,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$","description":"The ONE Theo label or relationship type you propose mapping the local type to. Resolved against the live schema on this call, never against a fixed list."},"local_edge_types":{"description":"The local edge-type TOKENS the candidate carries. Used only to answer whether a tier-1 anchor exists for the proposed target. Omit when the candidate carries none.","maxItems":64,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":64,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]{0,63}$"}}},"required":["local_type","local_kind","proposed_theo_label"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"find_bottlenecks","title":"Rank the methodology canon's connectors by stored betweenness centrality","description":"Returns the nodes with the highest betweenness centrality: the ones the most shortest paths run through, which is to say the connectors and chokepoints of the methodology graph. THE ANSWER DESCRIBES THEO'S OWN CANON (backend theo-canon): the scores are Theo's own canon's betweenness over its methodology projection, computed offline and exactly by the ingest tier and stored by one reviewed canon write. This tool reads the stored score and computes nothing, and it makes no network call. Each row carries label, name and score. label is the node's identity label, the first of Framework, MindrianCommand, DomainConcept, Phase, Technique or ProcessStep that it carries; name falls back to the node id. coverage counts the methodology population (total), how many nodes carry a score (scored) and how many score above zero (positive); a count the graph did not return is left out rather than reported as zero. as_of names the recompute the scores are AS OF (anchor_id, recompute_run_id, ingest_run_id, refreshed_at, projection_hash, scored_nodes), read from the GraphRagMeta node on every call; the scores are not live and change only at a reviewed canon write. limit is optional, defaults to 10 and is capped at 50; a larger value is refused rather than quietly reduced. remoteRowCount reports how many rows canon returned and limitEnforcedBy is always theo. WHEN NO RECOMPUTE HAS BEEN WRITTEN (as_of empty, or no node scored), the answer is a refusal with code not_scored and THE bottlenecks KEY IS ABSENT ENTIRELY, never an empty list; backend, coverage and as_of stay present to show why. An absent key means no scores exist to rank yet; it never means the graph has no bottlenecks.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"limit":{"description":"How many ranked nodes to return. Optional; defaults to 10, maximum 50. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":50}},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"find_whitespace","title":"Cluster the methodology canon into its stored communities, as candidate whitespace","description":"Returns the community structure of the methodology graph: clusters of nodes that connect densely to each other and sparsely to everything else. THE GAPS BETWEEN THOSE CLUSTERS ARE CANDIDATE WHITESPACE, NOT ASSERTED WHITESPACE. Theo reports the clusters and says no more; deciding that a gap is an opportunity is your judgement, not this tool's. THE ANSWER DESCRIBES THEO'S OWN CANON (backend theo-canon): the communities are Theo's own Louvain partition of its methodology projection, computed offline with a pinned seed by the ingest tier and stored by one reviewed canon write. This tool reads the stored community and computes nothing, and it makes no network call. Each community carries community_id (Theo's own integer, numbered from 0 by size), members (a count) and sample (up to four member names, ordered by stored PageRank then name). coverage counts the methodology population (total) and how many nodes carry a community (scored); a count the graph did not return is left out rather than reported as zero. as_of names the recompute the communities are AS OF (anchor_id, recompute_run_id, ingest_run_id, refreshed_at, projection_hash, scored_nodes), read from the GraphRagMeta node on every call; they are not live and change only at a reviewed canon write. min_size is optional, defaults to 3 and is capped at 1000. limit is optional, defaults to 10 and is capped at 50; a larger value is refused rather than quietly reduced. remoteRowCount reports how many communities canon returned and limitEnforcedBy is always theo. WHEN NO RECOMPUTE HAS BEEN WRITTEN (as_of empty, or no node carrying a community), the answer is a refusal with code not_scored and THE communities KEY IS ABSENT ENTIRELY, never an empty list; backend, coverage and as_of stay present to show why. An absent key means no partition exists yet; it never means the graph has no structure.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"min_size":{"description":"The smallest community to report, by member count. Optional; defaults to 3, maximum 1000. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":1000},"limit":{"description":"How many communities to return. Optional; defaults to 10, maximum 50. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":50}},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"commands_for_problem_type","title":"Find the MindrianOS commands for a problem type","description":"Returns the MindrianOS commands that reach one problem type through a Framework that addresses it, the job each command serves (jtbd), and the Framework connecting them, as rows of {command, jtbd, framework} ordered by command then framework. That is a TOTAL order, so the same call returns the same set every time: ordering by command alone let a limit cut an arbitrary one of two tied rows, which is the incumbent tie bug this order fixes. A command reached through two Frameworks appears once per Framework. Zero rows means no command reaches this rung through any Framework in canon, not an error. truncated is true when more rows existed than limit; coverage.commands_matched counts the distinct commands in the returned rows and coverage.commands_total is the live count of commands in canon. Recommendation lookup only: it never invokes a command. limit is optional, defaults to 50, maximum 100; a value outside 1 to 100 is refused, never clamped. find_commands_for_problem_type is retired in favour of this tool.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"problem_type":{"description":"One of the four canon DomainConcept ids. Case, spaces, hyphens and underscores are folded, so ill defined, Ill-Defined and illdefined all read as IllDefined; Trinity and Compass are meta-categories, not problem types. Resolve other wording through normalize_taxonomy_term first.","type":"string","enum":["UnDefined","IllDefined","WellDefined","Wicked"]},"limit":{"description":"How many rows to return. Optional; defaults to 50, maximum 100. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":100}},"required":["problem_type"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"framework_techniques","title":"Read one Framework's techniques, method tools and problem types","description":"Returns one Framework's techniques, each tagged with the edge that links it (via is USES_TECHNIQUE, EQUIPS_WITH or HAS_TECHNIQUE: the Brain spellings and Theo's own spelling are all read, because canon carries both), its method tools, and the problem types it addresses. coverage says, live, how many Frameworks carry any technique edge at all out of how many exist, so an empty techniques list reads as \"this layer is thin in canon\", not \"this framework has no techniques in practice\". The name is resolved through aliases first and the canonical name is returned as framework; an aliased spelling also returns frameworkIdentities. An unknown name refuses inside a success: the payload carries refusals and no techniques, method_tools, problem_types or coverage key at all, never an empty list. Takes an exact name, never a question. One of the three data tools a guided framework session is composed from.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"framework":{"type":"string","maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The name of ONE Framework, such as Six Thinking Hats, resolved through aliases to its canonical. Up to 128 characters. Matched exactly, never as a fragment."}},"required":["framework"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"structural_neighbours","title":"Rank the named nodes within two typed hops of one Framework","description":"Returns the Frameworks, Techniques, commands, problem types and other named nodes within two typed hops of ONE Framework, ranked by sharedPaths (how many distinct paths reach each one), then by name. Each neighbour carries its full labels list, its name and sharedPaths. The relationship types walked are listed in relationship_types on every answer; provenance edges (where a node was ingested from), chunk edges and mention edges are excluded on purpose, because one provenance record links thousands of nodes and would drown every real neighbour. Anchored on Frameworks only: the name is resolved through aliases and the canonical name is returned as anchor (the Brain accepted any node label; Theo guarantees unique names only on Framework). An unknown name refuses inside a success: the payload carries refusals and no anchor or neighbours key at all. An empty neighbours list for a known Framework is a real answer. limit is optional, defaults to 8, maximum 50; a larger value is refused, never reduced. Takes an exact name, never a question.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"framework":{"type":"string","maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The name of ONE Framework, such as Six Thinking Hats, resolved through aliases to its canonical. Up to 128 characters. Matched exactly, never as a fragment."},"limit":{"description":"How many neighbours to return. Optional; defaults to 8, maximum 50. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":50}},"required":["framework"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"rank_influence","title":"Rank Frameworks, Techniques and ProcessSteps by their stored PageRank score","description":"Returns Theo's own stored PageRank scores for Framework, Technique and ProcessStep nodes, highest first, as rows of labels (the FULL label list, since a node may carry more than one of the three), name (the node id when it has no name) and rank. SCORES ARE AS OF the recompute named in as_of, NOT LIVE: Theo's ingest tier computes them offline over Theo's methodology projection and one reviewed canon write stores them; this tool computes nothing, it reads the stored value. They change only when a reviewed canon write changes them. as_of RETURNS the GraphRagMeta node read on every call (anchor_id, recompute_run_id, ingest_run_id, refreshed_at, projection_hash, scored_nodes), NEVER a remembered run. AN EMPTY as_of MEANS no Theo recompute has been written yet, so every node reads as not scored: rows is empty and every scored count is zero. coverage reports, per label, how many nodes carry a score (scored) out of how many exist (total), measured on this call, so a name missing from rows is not scored, never unimportant; a node with no methodology edge is never scored. A count the graph did not return is left out rather than reported as zero, and a row with no usable score is dropped and counted in dropped_malformed. label is optional and is exactly one of Framework, Technique or ProcessStep; omit it for all three. limit is optional, defaults to 10, maximum 50; a value outside 1 to 50 is refused, never clamped. backend is theo-canon and scores_source is stored.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"label":{"description":"Optional. Exactly one of Framework, Technique or ProcessStep, to rank only nodes carrying that label. Omit to rank all three together.","type":"string","enum":["Framework","Technique","ProcessStep"]},"limit":{"description":"How many ranked nodes to return. Optional; defaults to 10, maximum 50. A value above the maximum is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":50}},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"visualize_chain","title":"Chain diagram data for one problem type","description":"Returns the recommended framework route for one problem type as diagram-ready data, in exactly recommend_chain's order (the ranking key names that order): steps of {step, framework, degree, rank when canon recorded one, commands}, where commands are the MindrianOS commands that use that framework. Where canon records a FEEDS_INTO edge from one step to the next, that step carries the edge confidence as edge_confidence; a missing arrow is omitted, not invented, and so is an edge with no recorded confidence. A problem type with no linked framework returns steps: [] as a success. Recommends, never triggers: it runs no command. The drawn diagram is a later phase; this tool returns the data and its text fallback only. max_steps is optional, 1 to 6, default 4.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"problem_type":{"description":"One of the four canon DomainConcept ids. Case, spaces, hyphens and underscores are folded, so ill defined, Ill-Defined and illdefined all read as IllDefined; Trinity and Compass are meta-categories, not problem types. Resolve other wording through normalize_taxonomy_term first.","type":"string","enum":["UnDefined","IllDefined","WellDefined","Wicked"]},"max_steps":{"description":"How many steps the route may hold, 1 to 6. Optional; defaults to 4. Out of range is refused, never clamped.","type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":6}},"required":["problem_type"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"visualize_framework_map","title":"Map data for one Framework's structural neighbourhood","description":"Returns one Framework's structural neighbourhood as map-ready data: steps, techniques, the Frameworks feeding in and out through FEEDS_INTO, the chapters it belongs to, and cases (the names of case studies and examples that illustrate it, including through its aliases), plus definition (the raw description, null when canon holds none). Each group is capped as caps states, and capped says whether anything was cut from any group; an empty cases list means no case in canon illustrates this Framework. groups_not_in_canon names any group canon cannot answer yet and is currently empty. There is no geometry: no shape is returned. The name is resolved through aliases first and the canonical name is returned as framework; an unknown name refuses inside a success with refusals and no group keys at all. The drawn map is a later phase; this tool returns the data and its text fallback only.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"framework":{"type":"string","maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$","description":"The name of ONE Framework, such as Six Thinking Hats, resolved through aliases to its canonical. Up to 128 characters. Matched exactly, never as a fragment."}},"required":["framework"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"persona_card","title":"Render one table member as card data and text","description":"Renders one member of the team around the navigator as structured card data plus a text fallback, from the handles you pass: a de Bono hat worn by a domain identity (name is the main domain, surname the specialization), with an optional archetype. Returns hat, name, surname (or null), archetype (or null), display_name (Dr. name surname) and card_text. Pure render: reads no graph, stores nothing, and makes no claim about canon. The free-text question and mission are NOT accepted and are refused if sent, because a content tool never receives free text; keep them in your own turn. The visual card is a later phase.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"hat":{"type":"string","enum":["white","red","black","yellow","green","blue"],"description":"The de Bono hat this member wears."},"name":{"type":"string","maxLength":60,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*(?: [A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*){0,2}$","description":"The member's main DOMAIN as a short generic handle, e.g. Oncology; never a person's real name or room content. One to three words of letters and hyphens, up to 60 characters."},"surname":{"description":"Optional. The member's SPECIALIZATION as a short generic handle, e.g. Immunotherapy; never a person's real name or room content. Same seal as name.","type":"string","maxLength":60,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*(?: [A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*){0,2}$"},"archetype":{"description":"Optional. One of the seven archetype tags.","type":"string","enum":["Founder","Researcher","Operator","Investor","Mentor","Domain Expert","Student"]}},"required":["hat","name"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"case_story","title":"Tell one real case study or example, by its name or by the Framework it illustrates","description":"Returns ONE real case (a CaseStudy or an Example) as title, summary, outcome, lesson, domain and framework (the Framework it illustrates), plus grounded and a live coverage count (frameworks_with_cases, cases_total). Takes exactly one of case_name or framework_name: neither or both refuses inside a success with refusals naming exactly-one-of and none of the data keys present. Both are exact names, never questions: a case name is matched exactly, never as a fragment (the older fragment match is not carried), and a framework name is resolved through aliases to its canonical, which is returned as framework; an aliased spelling also returns frameworkIdentities, and an unknown framework refuses inside a success with no data keys. When several cases match, the one with the fullest summary is returned. The incumbent fields actor, date, blindness and consequence are not carried: they were empty on every case. When the graph holds no matching case the answer is grounded: false with a note and the coverage counts, never an invented story.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"case_name":{"description":"The exact name of ONE case study or example. Up to 128 characters. Matched exactly, never as a fragment.","type":"string","maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$"},"framework_name":{"description":"The name of ONE Framework, such as Jobs To Be Done, resolved through aliases to its canonical. Up to 128 characters. Matched exactly, never as a fragment.","type":"string","maxLength":128,"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9 '(),.\\/-]{1,128}$"}},"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"text2cypher","title":"Execute caller-authored read-only Cypher","description":"The name is a misnomer, inherited from the original tool list and kept for compatibility: this tool translates nothing. It executes read-only Cypher you have ALREADY AUTHORED, behind the full guard stack. You hold the model and do the natural-language-to-Cypher translation; putting a model inside this tool would be a regression. Writes are refused. Refusal is decided on Neo4j's own EXPLAIN classification of your query plus an operator and procedure allow-list, before any session is opened, and there is no write tool in this catalog to fall back to. The two refusal codes mean different things: PLAN_REJECTED means a plan was produced and the policy refused it, so the fix is a DIFFERENT query; PLAN_UNAVAILABLE means no plan could be produced at all, so the fix is a CORRECT query. An answer larger than the row cap (100 rows) or the byte cap (262144 bytes) is NOT refused: it comes back as a success cut off at the cap and flagged `truncated: true` with `row_cap` or `byte_cap` and `returned`, so check `truncated` before treating the rows as complete, and add a LIMIT at or under the row cap when you need every row accounted for. A query that runs past the timeout is refused with TIMEOUT. This tool pays one extra round trip that the other seven content tools do not, so prefer a purpose-built tool when one fits and treat this as the escape hatch.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"cypher":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":4000,"description":"Already-authored read-only Cypher. Use $-prefixed parameters, never literals."},"params":{"default":{},"description":"The query parameters, matching the $-names used in the Cypher.","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["cypher"],"additionalProperties":false},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":true,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"},"outputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"truncated":{"description":"Present, and true, only when a cap fired and rows were cut off.","type":"boolean","const":true},"row_cap":{"description":"The row cap that fired. Set only on a row-cap truncation.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"byte_cap":{"description":"The byte cap that fired. Set only on a byte-cap truncation.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"returned":{"description":"How many rows this truncated answer carries.","type":"integer","minimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"rows":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"diagnostics":{"type":"object","properties":{"hasWarning":{"type":"boolean"},"notifications":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"gqlStatusObjects":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"warningGqlStatuses":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"required":["hasWarning","notifications","gqlStatusObjects","warningGqlStatuses"],"additionalProperties":false}},"required":["rows","diagnostics"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"room_bind","title":"Bind this session to a room","description":"Bind this MCP session to a room (D-03: explicit room wins; cwd auto-bind when launched inside a room; a structured binding-card signal on genuine ambiguity). The room this session binds to becomes the target for every subsequent write this session makes.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"room":{"description":"Explicit room slug to bind this session to. Wins over cwd auto-bind when supplied.","type":"string"},"sessionId":{"description":"Explicit sessionId override; defaults to this connection's session id.","type":"string"}}},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"graph_write","title":"Write one typed edge to the room graph","description":"Write a typed edge between two graph nodes. Routes EXCLUSIVELY through navigation.cjs (the single Part 9 chokepoint) -- never opens the graph store directly.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"source_id":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"target_id":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"edge_type":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"description":"One of navigation.cjs's allowed edge types (e.g. DEFERRED, REJECTED, INFORMS, FOLLOWS_FROM)."},"properties":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}},"read_version":{"description":"Optional CAS token (last_modified_at) from a prior read of source_id; a lost update is rejected as a conflict instead of silently clobbering.","anyOf":[{"type":"number"},{"type":"null"}]}},"required":["source_id","target_id","edge_type"]},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"gate_render","title":"Render a Mindrian gate decision card","description":"Render the Mindrian gate superset card (options + per-option descriptions + ranks + previews + single/multi-select) via the capability-detected 3-rung renderer ladder: MCP elicitation, Claude Code AskUserQuestion thin adapter, or headless structured text. Returns a gate_id minted into this server process's in-memory ledger, which gate_answer must reference to ratify; nothing is persisted and the id does not survive a restart.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"gate_id":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"header":{"type":"string"},"kind":{"description":"e.g. 'binding' for the D-04 once-per-session-on-ambiguity F.8 card; defaults to 'general'.","type":"string"},"ambiguous":{"description":"Only relevant to kind:'binding' -- an unambiguous context never fires the card.","type":"boolean"},"select_mode":{"type":"string","enum":["single","multi"]},"options":{"minItems":1,"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"label":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"description":{"type":"string"},"rank":{"type":"number"},"preview":{"type":"string"}},"required":["label"],"additionalProperties":{}}},"subject_node_id":{"description":"Opaque LOCAL room-graph node id, the card's subject. On an approve verdict for a kind 'general' card whose subject is a proposed claim node, gate_answer promotes that claim to confirmed through navigation.confirmNode; evidence nodes are never promoted. Canon Part 8: must never be a Brain identifier or room content.","type":"string","minLength":1},"evidence_node_ids":{"description":"Opaque LOCAL room-graph node ids, provenance only -- SOURCED_FROM targets on the written decision node, never promoted by gate_answer. Canon Part 8: must never be Brain identifiers or room content.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1}}},"required":["options"]},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"gate_answer","title":"Ratify a rendered gate decision","description":"Ratify a gate decision. Accepts the canonical { gate_id, chosen, verdict } payload; only ratifies a gate_id THIS server process minted through the shared ledger (lib/mcp/gate-ledger.cjs), including a gate id minted by a chain_run halt (T-198-10 spoofing guard, session-scoped). chosen must be an option id or an option label from the card that was actually minted; a chosen value outside that set is rejected with chosen_not_in_card_options before any write happens. On an approve verdict, routes the material write through lib/core/navigation.cjs (Part 9) -- never a direct DB write. An approve verdict ALSO writes a typed decision node with SOURCED_FROM provenance edges to the card's subject/evidence node ids, plus a USES_FRAMEWORK edge when the gate came from a chain halt with an active framework; the node is promoted to confirmed via navigation.confirmNode, recording the human APPROVE. When the gate_id was minted by a chain_run halt at a material step, gate_answer is ALSO the verb that resumes that chain: an approve verdict runs the halted step and continues the chain in this SAME call, returning the result nested under chain_result; if the continued chain halts again at a later material step, chain_result carries the next gate_id to answer the same way. The gate is single-use, so a second gate_answer for the same gate_id, or a chain_run call threaded with the same answer afterward, is refused with unknown_or_expired_gate and nothing re-runs.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"gate_id":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"chosen":{"minItems":1,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1}},"verdict":{"type":"string","enum":["approve","reject","defer"]}},"required":["gate_id","chosen","verdict"]},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}},{"name":"chain_run","title":"Run a framework chain, halting at the first material step","description":"Execute an ordered framework-name chain server-side, WRAPPING the shipped lib/core/chain-executor.cjs::runChain (Phase 166) -- mints no second executor. Runs the autonomous_safe prefix (posture resolved from the ONE shared autonomy authority in lib/core/recipe-maps.cjs, never re-derived); HALTS at the first material step and returns a gate_id minted into the shared gate ledger instead of executing it unattended. To resume, call gate_answer with that gate_id: it ratifies the decision AND runs the approved step and continues the chain in that SAME call, returning the real execution result. Do NOT then thread the answer back into chain_run -- the gate is single-use, so the second caller for that gate_id is refused with unknown_or_expired_gate and nothing re-runs. `chosen` must be an option id or an option label from the returned card, or the resume is rejected without executing the step. Only the session that halted the chain can resume it; a different session's gate_answer for the same gate_id is rejected. An approve verdict executes the halted step and continues; reject/defer leaves it unexecuted.","inputSchema":{"type":"object","properties":{"chain":{"description":"Ordered framework-name chain to resolve and run. Required to START a chain; omit when resuming via gate_answer.","minItems":1,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1}},"gate_answer":{"description":"The DIRECT programmatic resume path for a caller driving chainRun in-process (not through the gate_answer MCP tool) -- it consumes the SAME single-use ledger entry and runs the SAME execution path the gate_answer tool invokes. `chosen` must match an option id or label from the halt gate's own card, and must come from the SAME session that halted the chain, or the resume is rejected before the step runs. A caller uses ONE verb or the other for a given gate_id, never both: whichever of gate_answer or this parameter consumes the entry first is the only one that executes; the other is refused with unknown_or_expired_gate.","type":"object","properties":{"gate_id":{"type":"string","minLength":1},"chosen":{"minItems":1,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1}},"verdict":{"type":"string","enum":["approve","reject","defer"]}},"required":["gate_id","chosen","verdict"],"additionalProperties":{}},"subject_node_id":{"description":"Opaque LOCAL room-graph node id, provenance only. Carried onto the halt card if this chain halts at a material step; nothing consumes it yet. Not used on the gate_answer resume branch (that path consumes a ledger entry that already carries it). Canon Part 8: must never be a Brain identifier or room content.","type":"string","minLength":1},"evidence_node_ids":{"description":"Opaque LOCAL room-graph node ids, provenance only. Carried onto the halt card if this chain halts at a material step; nothing consumes it yet. Not used on the gate_answer resume branch (that path consumes a ledger entry that already carries it). Canon Part 8: must never be Brain identifiers or room content.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1}}}},"annotations":{"readOnlyHint":false,"openWorldHint":false},"execution":{"taskSupport":"forbidden"}}]},"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1}

